WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.395
Lesson 5.6.

00:00:02.395 --> 00:00:06.746
Beyond Ethereum: Solana, Bitcoin and other ecosystems.

00:00:06.746 --> 00:00:09.679
By the end, you'll be able to operate

00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:13.771
safely on non-EVM chains, and understand exactly how

00:00:13.771 --> 00:00:16.781
Bitcoin actually gets used inside DeFi.

00:00:18.081 --> 00:00:19.371
Here's the plan.

00:00:19.371 --> 00:00:22.642
Solana, a genuinely separate ecosystem, with its

00:00:22.642 --> 00:00:25.379
own wallets, its own address format, and

00:00:25.379 --> 00:00:27.481
its own gas token entirely.

00:00:27.481 --> 00:00:29.780
Bitcoin in DeFi, and why it's almost

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.079
always used as a wrapped token, with

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:34.181
its own distinct trust model.

00:00:34.181 --> 00:00:38.088
Other ecosystems, and applying this module's own dependency

00:00:38.088 --> 00:00:40.471
map to every single one of them.

00:00:40.471 --> 00:00:42.911
And a full worked example, choosing how

00:00:42.911 --> 00:00:45.411
to actually lend one bitcoin on Ethereum.

00:00:46.711 --> 00:00:49.431
Solana: a genuinely separate ecosystem.

00:00:50.731 --> 00:00:54.251
Here's exactly what makes Solana genuinely separate, not

00:00:54.251 --> 00:00:58.626
simply another Ethereum-compatible chain wearing a different name.

00:00:58.626 --> 00:01:02.899
It's a high-throughput chain with its own dedicated wallets,

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:06.961
like Phantom or Solflare, its own distinct address format

00:01:06.961 --> 00:01:10.744
entirely, and very low fees, paid specifically in its

00:01:10.744 --> 00:01:14.476
own native token, SOL, not in Ethereum's own ETH.

00:01:14.476 --> 00:01:17.333
Its tokens follow their own separate

00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:21.116
standard, S.P.L., not Ethereum's familiar ERC-20.

00:01:22.416 --> 00:01:26.095
Worth being precise about a distinction that trips people

00:01:26.095 --> 00:01:30.028
up constantly: not every chain outside Ethereum's own mainnet

00:01:30.028 --> 00:01:33.834
is actually non-E.V.M. Layer twos, like the rollups covered

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:37.640
back in Lesson five point two, are still E.V.M.-compatible,

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.192
meaning your same Ethereum address, and often your same

00:01:41.192 --> 00:01:43.966
wallet, genuinely works there directly.

00:01:43.966 --> 00:01:48.661
Solana specifically is different: a genuinely separate, non-E.V.M.

00:01:48.661 --> 00:01:52.586
architecture, needing its own dedicated wallet entirely.

00:01:53.886 --> 00:01:56.646
Worth being completely direct about the single

00:01:56.646 --> 00:01:59.237
most important consequence of all this.

00:01:59.237 --> 00:02:02.243
Your Ethereum address genuinely does not work

00:02:02.243 --> 00:02:05.352
on Solana, at all, under any circumstances.

00:02:05.352 --> 00:02:09.722
Sending assets between these two ecosystems specifically requires

00:02:09.722 --> 00:02:12.966
a bridge, or a centralised exchange, exactly the

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:16.475
same category of infrastructure risk covered back in

00:02:16.475 --> 00:02:19.852
Lesson five point one, just connecting a different

00:02:19.852 --> 00:02:21.507
pair of chains this time.

00:02:22.807 --> 00:02:26.017
Worth also naming priority fees specifically, since

00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:29.103
they're a genuinely distinct Solana concept worth

00:02:29.103 --> 00:02:31.502
knowing before you transact there.

00:02:31.502 --> 00:02:34.959
During busy periods, you can pay an additional priority

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:37.736
fee, on top of the base fee, specifically to

00:02:37.736 --> 00:02:41.687
have your transaction processed faster, ahead of others willing

00:02:41.687 --> 00:02:43.847
to pay less for that same priority.

00:02:45.147 --> 00:02:46.237
Quick check.

00:02:46.237 --> 00:02:49.192
Does your Ethereum address actually work on Solana?

00:02:53.612 --> 00:02:56.446
The answer: no. It's a genuinely different

00:02:56.446 --> 00:02:59.280
ecosystem, with its own address format and

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.587
its own dedicated wallets entirely.

00:03:02.887 --> 00:03:04.142
Bitcoin in DeFi.

00:03:05.442 --> 00:03:08.849
Here's exactly why Bitcoin itself can't simply run a

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:12.557
DeFi app directly, and what actually happens instead.

00:03:12.557 --> 00:03:16.168
Bitcoin's own base layer genuinely doesn't support the

00:03:16.168 --> 00:03:19.947
kind of smart-contract logic DeFi apps actually need.

00:03:19.947 --> 00:03:22.791
So B.T.C. gets used instead as a wrapped

00:03:22.791 --> 00:03:26.191
token, on other chains, backed specifically by a

00:03:26.191 --> 00:03:29.382
custodian, a group of signers, or a dedicated

00:03:29.382 --> 00:03:33.892
protocol, or alternatively used through a Bitcoin-specific layer

00:03:33.892 --> 00:03:37.222
two or sidechain built for exactly this purpose.

00:03:38.522 --> 00:03:42.193
Worth being completely precise about the single sentence

00:03:42.193 --> 00:03:45.285
this entire topic actually reduces to, since it

00:03:45.285 --> 00:03:48.677
directly echoes this module's own bridge lesson.

00:03:48.677 --> 00:03:51.620
Your wrapped B.T.C. is only ever as good as

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.366
whoever actually holds the real Bitcoin backing it, and

00:03:55.366 --> 00:03:58.542
exactly how you'd genuinely redeem it back.

00:03:58.542 --> 00:04:01.485
That's the exact same trust-model question from

00:04:01.485 --> 00:04:03.938
Lesson five point one, just asked about

00:04:03.938 --> 00:04:06.207
a different specific asset this time.

00:04:07.507 --> 00:04:10.762
Here's what those different backing arrangements actually

00:04:10.762 --> 00:04:14.074
look like, mechanically, since custodial and decentralised

00:04:14.074 --> 00:04:16.731
wrapping work in genuinely different ways.

00:04:16.731 --> 00:04:20.048
A custodial wrapper means one specific company holds

00:04:20.048 --> 00:04:22.990
the real Bitcoin directly, in its own custody,

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.731
and mints the wrapped token against it.

00:04:25.731 --> 00:04:29.225
A decentralised wrapper instead means a broader group

00:04:29.225 --> 00:04:32.848
of signers, or a protocol's own mechanism, collectively

00:04:32.848 --> 00:04:35.501
holds and secures that same real Bitcoin.

00:04:36.801 --> 00:04:37.891
Quick check.

00:04:37.891 --> 00:04:42.016
What actually determines a wrapped B.T.C. token's real safety?

00:04:46.436 --> 00:04:49.511
The answer: who genuinely holds the real Bitcoin

00:04:49.511 --> 00:04:52.210
behind it, and exactly how it can actually

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.281
be redeemed, its own trust model.

00:04:55.581 --> 00:04:58.336
Other ecosystems, same dependency map.

00:04:59.636 --> 00:05:02.859
Here's exactly how to treat any other ecosystem

00:05:02.859 --> 00:05:06.419
you encounter, whether it's a Cosmos chain connected

00:05:06.419 --> 00:05:10.045
through I.B.C., a Move-based chain, or something else

00:05:10.045 --> 00:05:12.561
entirely you haven't seen before.

00:05:12.561 --> 00:05:15.163
Each one genuinely has its own wallets,

00:05:15.163 --> 00:05:17.439
its own fee structure, and its own

00:05:17.439 --> 00:05:20.601
bridges, exactly like Solana and Bitcoin do.

00:05:20.601 --> 00:05:23.209
And this module's own dependency map, from

00:05:23.209 --> 00:05:25.636
its very first lesson, applies to every

00:05:25.636 --> 00:05:27.941
single one of them, without exception.

00:05:29.241 --> 00:05:32.303
Worth naming I.B.C. specifically, since it's a

00:05:32.303 --> 00:05:35.821
genuinely different approach from the bridges covered

00:05:35.821 --> 00:05:38.076
back in Lesson five point one.

00:05:38.076 --> 00:05:42.729
I.B.C. lets Cosmos chains communicate through a shared, standardised

00:05:42.729 --> 00:05:46.371
protocol, rather than each pair of chains needing its

00:05:46.371 --> 00:05:49.841
own separate, custom bridge built between them.

00:05:49.841 --> 00:05:53.718
That's a meaningfully different trust model worth understanding on

00:05:53.718 --> 00:05:56.727
its own terms, not simply assuming it works exactly

00:05:56.727 --> 00:05:59.331
like the bridges from earlier in this module.

00:06:00.631 --> 00:06:03.252
Worth being direct about the actual rules

00:06:03.252 --> 00:06:06.560
that stay identical, everywhere, regardless of which

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:09.856
specific ecosystem you're actually operating in.

00:06:09.856 --> 00:06:11.838
Use the official wallet, from the

00:06:11.838 --> 00:06:14.061
official site, every single time.

00:06:14.061 --> 00:06:16.391
Send a small test transaction first.

00:06:16.391 --> 00:06:19.321
Confirm you're genuinely on the correct network.

00:06:19.321 --> 00:06:21.581
And hold that network's own gas token,

00:06:21.581 --> 00:06:23.726
on hand, before you actually need it.

00:06:25.026 --> 00:06:28.386
Here's how to actually verify you're genuinely using

00:06:28.386 --> 00:06:31.429
the official wallet for a new ecosystem, rather

00:06:31.429 --> 00:06:34.391
than assuming a top search result is safe.

00:06:34.391 --> 00:06:37.945
Get the download link directly from the ecosystem's own

00:06:37.945 --> 00:06:41.926
official documentation or website, never from a search ad.

00:06:41.926 --> 00:06:44.408
Confirm the wallet's own publisher name

00:06:44.408 --> 00:06:47.687
matches what that documentation actually states.

00:06:47.687 --> 00:06:50.058
And send a small test transaction first,

00:06:50.058 --> 00:06:52.372
exactly like every other lesson in this

00:06:52.372 --> 00:06:54.107
module has already taught you.

00:06:55.407 --> 00:06:56.497
Quick check.

00:06:56.497 --> 00:06:59.682
What's Solana's own native gas token specifically called?

00:07:04.102 --> 00:07:05.357
The answer: SOL.

00:07:06.657 --> 00:07:07.662
Worked example.

00:07:08.962 --> 00:07:11.594
Here's the actual choice, laid out fully,

00:07:11.594 --> 00:07:14.777
for genuinely lending one bitcoin on Ethereum.

00:07:14.777 --> 00:07:17.983
A custodial wrapper means trusting one specific

00:07:17.983 --> 00:07:20.988
company, directly, to actually hold the real

00:07:20.988 --> 00:07:23.492
B.T.C. behind your wrapped token.

00:07:23.492 --> 00:07:26.584
A decentralised wrapper instead means trusting a

00:07:26.584 --> 00:07:29.108
broader signer set, or a protocol's own

00:07:29.108 --> 00:07:32.137
mechanism, rather than one single company alone.

00:07:33.437 --> 00:07:36.065
Worth naming the third option too, since it's

00:07:36.065 --> 00:07:38.992
the one most likely to be overlooked entirely.

00:07:38.992 --> 00:07:41.625
Not wrapping at all means simply keeping

00:07:41.625 --> 00:07:44.644
your Bitcoin as Bitcoin, forgoing the specific

00:07:44.644 --> 00:07:48.027
yield this lending opportunity offers, entirely.

00:07:48.027 --> 00:07:50.627
That's a genuinely legitimate choice, not a

00:07:50.627 --> 00:07:53.699
failure to act, specifically when neither wrapper's

00:07:53.699 --> 00:07:55.767
trust model actually satisfies you.

00:07:57.067 --> 00:07:59.352
Here's exactly what to actually do with

00:07:59.352 --> 00:08:02.038
these three options, rather than picking based

00:08:02.038 --> 00:08:04.052
on the advertised yield alone.

00:08:04.052 --> 00:08:06.776
Write down each option's own specific trust

00:08:06.776 --> 00:08:09.367
model, explicitly, in your own words.

00:08:09.367 --> 00:08:13.179
Cap whatever wrapped B.T.C. exposure you do decide to

00:08:13.179 --> 00:08:16.709
take, treating it as exactly the same bridge risk

00:08:16.709 --> 00:08:19.692
covered back in Lesson five point one.

00:08:19.692 --> 00:08:22.091
And only then compare the actual yield

00:08:22.091 --> 00:08:24.552
on offer against that fully-priced risk.

00:08:25.852 --> 00:08:27.312
Here's your checklist.

00:08:27.312 --> 00:08:31.127
Do it for every non-E.V.M. chain you actually use.

00:08:31.127 --> 00:08:34.637
Keep a separate, official wallet for each one, specifically.

00:08:34.637 --> 00:08:36.699
Hold that chain's own gas token on

00:08:36.699 --> 00:08:39.002
hand, before you actually need it.

00:08:39.002 --> 00:08:42.521
And know your wrapped-B.T.C. trust model, precisely,

00:08:42.521 --> 00:08:45.177
and keep that exposure genuinely capped.

00:08:46.477 --> 00:08:47.722
Let's recap.

00:08:47.722 --> 00:08:50.820
Solana is a genuinely separate ecosystem: its

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.784
own wallets, address format, and gas token;

00:08:53.784 --> 00:08:57.317
your Ethereum address simply doesn't work there.

00:08:57.317 --> 00:09:00.425
Bitcoin gets used in DeFi almost entirely as a

00:09:00.425 --> 00:09:04.195
wrapped token, and that wrapper's real safety comes down

00:09:04.195 --> 00:09:07.537
to its own trust model, exactly like a bridge.

00:09:07.537 --> 00:09:11.837
Other ecosystems, Cosmos, Move-based chains, and beyond, all

00:09:11.837 --> 00:09:15.662
get this same module's own dependency map applied.

00:09:15.662 --> 00:09:18.761
And the same four rules hold everywhere: official

00:09:18.761 --> 00:09:22.417
wallet, test transaction, correct network, gas token ready.

00:09:23.717 --> 00:09:27.231
Worth closing on the actual point this lesson proves,

00:09:27.231 --> 00:09:30.029
one more time, since it's easy to think of

00:09:30.029 --> 00:09:33.582
non-Ethereum chains as an entirely separate topic.

00:09:33.582 --> 00:09:37.299
Everything this module already taught you, the dependency

00:09:37.299 --> 00:09:40.761
map, bridge trust models, oracle risk, contract risk,

00:09:40.761 --> 00:09:43.432
doesn't reset at Ethereum's own edge.

00:09:43.432 --> 00:09:46.843
It's the same map, applied to unfamiliar territory, not

00:09:46.843 --> 00:09:49.341
a new set of rules to learn from scratch.

00:09:50.641 --> 00:09:53.833
Use a separate official wallet per chain, hold each

00:09:53.833 --> 00:09:57.631
chain's gas token, and know your wrapped-BTC trust model.

00:09:57.631 --> 00:10:00.597
Next up, Lesson 5.7: operating across

00:10:00.597 --> 00:10:03.876
chains, gas, routes and chain abstraction.
